## Residue of $f(z)=\dfrac{\cot z\,\coth z}{z^3}$ at $z=0$

**Laurent series of the factors.** Recall the standard expansions around $z=0$:

$$\cot z = \frac{1}{z}-\frac{z}{3}-\frac{z^{3}}{45}-\frac{2z^{5}}{945}-\cdots,$$

$$\coth z = \frac{1}{z}+\frac{z}{3}-\frac{z^{3}}{45}+\frac{2z^{5}}{945}-\cdots.$$

(The second follows from $\coth z = -i\cot(iz)$, which flips the sign of every odd-power coefficient.)

**Parity observation.** Both $\cot z$ and $\coth z$ are odd functions, so their product $g(z)=\cot z\,\coth z$ is **even**. Hence its Laurent expansion contains only even powers of $z$:

$$g(z)=\frac{c_{-2}}{z^{2}}+c_{0}+c_{2}z^{2}+c_{4}z^{4}+\cdots.$$

**Computing the coefficients.** Write $g(z)=A(z)B(z)$ with

$$A(z)=\sum a_{k}z^{k},\quad a_{-1}=1,\ a_{1}=-\tfrac13,\ a_{3}=-\tfrac1{45},\ a_{5}=-\tfrac{2}{945},\dots$$

$$B(z)=\sum b_{k}z^{k},\quad b_{-1}=1,\ b_{1}=\tfrac13,\ b_{3}=-\tfrac1{45},\ b_{5}=\tfrac{2}{945},\dots$$

- **$z^{-2}$ term:** $a_{-1}b_{-1}=1\cdot 1=1$, so $c_{-2}=1$.

- **$z^{0}$ term:** $a_{-1}b_{1}+a_{1}b_{-1}=1\cdot\tfrac13+(-\tfrac13)\cdot1=0$, so $c_{0}=0$ (consistent with parity).

- **$z^{2}$ term:**
$$c_{2}=a_{-1}b_{3}+a_{1}b_{1}+a_{3}b_{-1}=1\!\left(-\tfrac1{45}\right)+\!\left(-\tfrac13\right)\!\left(\tfrac13\right)+\!\left(-\tfrac1{45}\right)\!1=-\tfrac1{45}-\tfrac19-\tfrac1{45}=-\tfrac{2}{45}-\tfrac{5}{45}=-\tfrac{7}{45}.$$

Thus

$$\cot z\,\coth z=\frac{1}{z^{2}}-\frac{7}{45}\,z^{2}+O(z^{4}).$$

**Dividing by $z^{3}$.**

$$f(z)=\frac{\cot z\,\coth z}{z^{3}}=\frac{1}{z^{5}}+0\cdot\frac{1}{z^{3}}-\frac{7}{45}\cdot\frac{1}{z}+O(z).$$

The residue is the coefficient of $z^{-1}$:

$$\boxed{\operatorname{Res}_{z=0} f(z)=-\frac{7}{45}}.$$

